WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Brilliant, a problem solving website

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.569
that teaches you to think like an engineer.

00:00:07.569 --> 00:00:10.349
Simple malfunctions have plagued space flight
from its inception.

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:14.680
It goes without saying that pushing things
into space with controlled explosions is a

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:19.039
treacherous affair, and many rocket launches
have failed over simple problems.

00:00:19.039 --> 00:00:23.420
The three crew members of Soyuz 11 became
the first and only humans to die in space

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:27.630
after the explosive bolts holding the service
module and descent module together fired,

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.330
a routine procedure, but the bolts were designed
to fire sequentially, one after the other.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:37.010
On this occasion they fired simultaneously
and the combined explosive force loosened

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:42.390
a seal separating the crew from the vacuum
of space, within a minute the crew were dead.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:47.040
More recently, Space X’s first launch failed
simply because they left their rocket exposed

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.020
to sea air for too long, allowing it to rust
an aluminium nut securing the fuel pump inlet

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.630
pressure transducer.

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:57.400
When it failed fuel began to leak down the
outside of the engine and thrust chamber,

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.690
and caught fire.

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:03.170
The fire caused a loss of pneumatic pressure,
forcing valves to close, and thus cutting

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:04.330
thrust.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:08.860
Space X’s first rocket fell out of the sky
over a simple oversight of aluminiums weakness

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.140
to corrosion.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:14.250
After this flight Space X switched to rust
resistant stainless steel fasteners, which

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:18.140
are actually cheaper to begin with, but about
2 and a half times heavier.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.189
There is very little margin for error in space
flight.

00:01:21.189 --> 00:01:25.710
Tiny oversights like this lead to catastrophic
failure, and this is exactly what happened

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:30.880
to, what was intended to be the first successful
planetary fly-by in human history, the Mariner

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:31.880
1.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.159
On July 22nd 1962.

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:36.090
Mariner 1 launched from Cape Canaveral.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:40.119
On a mission intended to fly by Venus and
collect data on its temperature and atmosphere.

00:01:40.119 --> 00:01:46.420
But just 293 seconds into its flight the Range
Safety Officer gave the command to self destruct

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:51.200
the rocket over an unpopulated area of the
Atlantic, seeing that it’s trajectory was

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.299
taking it to a crash landing possibly in busy
shipping lanes or land.

00:01:55.299 --> 00:01:59.649
Despite the booster performing nominally,
the rocket took an unprogrammed yaw-lift turn

00:01:59.649 --> 00:02:01.869
and steering commands were unresponsive.

00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:06.180
With just 6 seconds remaining before the Mariner
1 probe was scheduled to separate and ground

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:11.090
control would be lost, that officer made the
decision to send the self destruct command.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:15.200
To understand what went wrong we first need
to learn a little about how the Atlas Agena

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.230
rocket, which the Mariner 1 probe was perched
upon, worked.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:23.720
The Atlas booster rocket used two radar systems
to maintain its trajectory along it’s intended

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:24.720
route.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.170
A rate system, which measured it’s velocity
using doppler shift measurements from a ground

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.819
based beacon , and a track system which measured
it’s distance and angle relative to an antenna

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:35.150
located near its launch site.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:38.700
Shortly into the launch the first problem
occurred, the rate system failed.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:42.791
The track system should have been capable
of handling navigation by itself, the two

00:02:42.791 --> 00:02:47.430
systems working together formed some redundancy
in measurements, to allow for a small margin

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:48.430
of error.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:53.090
This is why previous launches using the Atlas
Agena rockets went off without a hitch, but

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.209
on this occasion the Rate System failed.

00:02:55.209 --> 00:02:59.379
The code designed to interpret the distance
and angle measurements had a simple error.

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:03.170
This is the notation the position measurement
should have had, and this was the notation

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:04.769
it actually had in the code.

00:03:04.769 --> 00:03:06.830
It was missing an overbar.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:11.019
This dot stands for the first derivative of
the position, which gives us the velocity,,

00:03:11.019 --> 00:03:14.690
and the bar simply means to take the average
of the values.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:19.720
Giving a smoothed value for the velocity,
which helps remove any fluxuations of measurement.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:23.750
Because this bar was missing the rocket guidance
computer was being fed erratic information

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:28.519
about the rockets velocity and it began trying
to compensate for them, leading to actual

00:03:28.519 --> 00:03:29.830
erratic flight.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:33.940
This was the point the range officer recognised
something was wrong and initiated the self

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:35.440
destruct command.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:39.210
An incredibly simple mistake that should have
been caught, but these were the early days

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:40.210
of programming.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:44.470
We didn’t have fancy computer interfaces
for writing code with compiling software and

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:45.710
error detection.

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:50.330
The code for the guidance computer was mostly
written in Fortran, an early coding language.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:54.780
Fortran code, which stood for “Formula Translation”
was used to translate mathematical equations

00:03:54.780 --> 00:04:04.190
into code, and was handwritten or typed on
a typewriter.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:06.299
This is where punch cards came into play.

00:04:06.299 --> 00:04:10.420
Punch cards are thick rectangular pieces of
paper with long rows of repeating numbers,

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:14.000
0 through 9, running down their length in
eighty columns.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.489
Each card held one line of code, with each
column representing 1 character of code in

00:04:18.489 --> 00:04:19.489
that line.

00:04:19.489 --> 00:04:23.130
To represent a number you simply punched out
the corresponding number in the sheet.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:27.190
To represent letters and symbols required
you to punch out additional holes.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:31.630
This sheet has all the possible hole punch
combinations required for Fortran coding to

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:32.840
give you an example.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:36.860
This was done on a card punch machine where
you had to manually type out each line of

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:38.430
the code again.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:42.650
Once you had converted all you code to punch
cards, the cards would be sent through a compiler,

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:44.490
where they would be converted to binary.

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:48.770
If a hole was present, a circuit would be
completed through a particular set of contacts,

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:51.180
and thus a binary signal was produced.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:56.110
The compiler then produced a new set of cards
that the computer could actually understand.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:00.040
Obviously this method of coding is much more
tedious and difficult to fact check when compared

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:01.990
to present day user interfaces.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.699
It’s no excuse, but obviously mistakes were
much easier to make and this particular mistake

00:05:06.699 --> 00:05:13.699
ended up costing NASA 18.5 million dollars
in 1962, which is about 150 million dollars

00:05:13.699 --> 00:05:14.699
today.

00:05:14.699 --> 00:05:18.750
The code was corrected, and just a month later
Mariner 2 was launched, on its 3 and a half

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.539
month flight to Venus.

00:05:20.539 --> 00:05:25.449
On its way Mariner 2 detected solar wind for
the first time, the constant stream of charged

00:05:25.449 --> 00:05:27.600
particles emitted from the Sun.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.830
It measured interplanetary dust levels, and
during its flyby of Venus revealed information

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.250
about the planet’s temperature and atmosphere.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:37.910
Mariner 2 is now floating somewhere in orbit
around the Sun.

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:41.760
Learning from our mistakes is a common theme
in human history, which is why I find Brilliant’s

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.759
style of teaching complicated subjects fantastic.

00:05:44.759 --> 00:05:46.509
Take this course on classical mechanics.

00:05:46.509 --> 00:05:50.880
It takes you through the fundamentals of the
physics of motion, an essential skill for

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.169
any rocket engineer.

00:05:52.169 --> 00:05:56.130
It will give you short digestible overviews
of a problem, and then give you a problem

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:57.630
of your own to solve.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:05:59.710
If you get it correct, you advance forward.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:04.069
If you get it wrong however, it will tell
you exactly why and teach you more about the

00:06:04.069 --> 00:06:05.069
subject.

00:06:05.069 --> 00:06:08.750
Allowing you to learn from your own mistakes,
and hopefully not repeat them.

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:12.819
This is encapsulated in their principles of
learning - by allowing for failure and acknowledging

00:06:12.819 --> 00:06:18.080
it happens, one is open to correcting misconceptions
and errors, which furthers understanding of

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:19.080
the topics.

